(*
expect:
hash nil
trial false
running false
int refused
nil refused
unfrozen refused
stage refused
bad sig refused
activate refused
finished
*)
(* rete#25 — the ota seam with NO runner environment, which is every host run of
   this tree and every node launched bare. The claim is not "the prims exist";
   it is that their honest answers are ORDINARY VALUES a caller can branch on
   with no handler in sight — nil for "I do not know what I am running", false
   for "no, this is not a trial boot", false for "no, that is not me".

   There is no env: header here ON PURPOSE. Absence is the default (ota.c's
   ota_env answers NULL for an unset variable), so this path is exercised by
   every run rather than only when someone remembers a knob — the asymmetry
   21_wallclock/02 documents, for the same reason.

   The first half asks `otaIsRunning:` with a well-formed 32-byte digest, not
   with junk: the interesting failure there is not "a bad argument is rejected"
   but "a perfectly good hash still cannot match, because nothing is known to be
   running". A seam that answered true would let a node skip a push it has never
   applied.

   The second half is the opposite question and a separate claim — that a
   MALFORMED argument is refused rather than fatal. See the comment above it. *)
class OtaEnvAbsent packageUsing: manifest = () (
  public main: platform args: args = (
    | h probe |
    h:: platform otaRunningHash.
    h == nil
      ifTrue:  [ 'hash nil' println ]
      ifFalse: [ ('hash unexpectedly ' , h printHex) println ].

    platform otaTrial
      ifTrue:  [ 'trial true' println ]
      ifFalse: [ 'trial false' println ].

    (* sha256 of the empty string — a real digest, so the fixture is checkable. *)
    probe:: platform ByteArray fromHex: 'e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855'.
    (platform otaIsRunning: probe)
      ifTrue:  [ 'running true' println ]
      ifFalse: [ 'running false' println ].

    (* A HOSTILE ARGUMENT MUST NOT KILL THE NODE. Once PortaBridge wires this
       seam up, the argument to each of these comes out of a gateway-written
       `goal/` record, so "not a Bytes" is a peer's mistake and must be an
       answer. It nearly was not: the accessors used to send `rawLeaf` to the
       argument before any guard could run, and `rawLeaf` exists only on Bytes,
       so a malformed field reached dispatch.c's abort() — one datagram, one
       dead node. Every line below aborted the VM before the guard was added.

       Reaching `finished` at all is therefore the real assertion here; the
       printed words only say which arm answered. *)
    (platform otaIsRunning: 42)
      ifTrue:  [ 'int accepted — GUARD MISSING' println ]
      ifFalse: [ 'int refused' println ].
    (platform otaIsRunning: nil)
      ifTrue:  [ 'nil accepted — GUARD MISSING' println ]
      ifFalse: [ 'nil refused' println ].

    (* An UNFROZEN ByteArray is the interesting one: it is byte-shaped and has a
       raw leaf, so it would have sailed through a guard written as "does it
       have bytes?". It is refused because its owner can still mutate it
       underneath a blocking fetch, and because Bytes is reachable only through
       a real freeze copy. *)
    (platform otaIsRunning: (platform ByteArray new: 32))
      ifTrue:  [ 'unfrozen accepted — GUARD MISSING' println ]
      ifFalse: [ 'unfrozen refused' println ].

    (* The guard fires BEFORE any I/O: a refused stage touches no socket, so
       this line costs nothing even though the seam is otherwise blocking. *)
    (platform otaStage: 42 sig: nil host: '127.0.0.1' port: 1)
      ifTrue:  [ 'stage accepted — GUARD MISSING' println ]
      ifFalse: [ 'stage refused' println ].

    (* A malformed SIGNATURE refuses the whole call too, rather than being
       dropped to nil — a staged image with its provenance quietly removed would
       reach the runner unsigned, with nothing left to check. Absent and
       malformed are different things. The hash here is well-formed, so only the
       sig can be what refuses this. *)
    (platform otaStage: probe sig: 42 host: '127.0.0.1' port: 1)
      ifTrue:  [ 'bad sig accepted — GUARD MISSING' println ]
      ifFalse: [ 'bad sig refused' println ].

    (* otaActivate: answers self and cannot report a refusal, so the proof is
       structural: with a valid hash this call would write ready.json and exit
       the process with 42, and with a bad one it must simply return. The next
       line printing at all is the whole assertion. *)
    platform otaActivate: 42 sig: nil.
    'activate refused' println.

    (* No handler anywhere above: reaching this line is the proof that a missing
       runner is an answer and not a raise. *)
    'finished' println.
  )
) : (
)
